Medical Appointment No-Show Analysis
Healthcare Data Analytics Project Using SQL and Statistical Analysis

Tools Used: SQL (MySQL), Microsoft Excel, SPSS Statistical Workflow (completed in Jamovi), GitHub

Project Overview

Missed medical appointments are a significant challenge for healthcare organizations because they reduce operational efficiency, increase healthcare costs, and delay patient care. Understanding the factors associated with appointment attendance can help healthcare providers improve scheduling practices and develop strategies to reduce appointment no-show rates.

The objective of this project was to identify demographic, clinical, and appointment-related factors associated with appointment attendance using a real-world healthcare dataset. The project demonstrates an end-to-end healthcare data analytics workflow, including data cleaning, exploratory data analysis, statistical hypothesis testing, and predictive modelling.

Project Objectives

The objectives of this project were to:

Clean and validate a healthcare appointment dataset using SQL.
Perform exploratory data analysis to understand patient and appointment characteristics.
Summarize patient demographics using descriptive statistics.
Examine relationships between patient characteristics and appointment attendance using Chi-square tests.
Compare the mean age of patients who attended appointments with those who missed appointments using an independent samples t-test.
Develop a binary logistic regression model to identify independent predictors of appointment attendance.
